  "aiOverview": {
    "content": "\"Kid\" originates from the 12th-century Middle English kide, derived from Old Norse kith, initially meaning a young goat. By the 16th century, it was used informally for a child or young person. It acts as a synonym for child, teenager, youngster, or baby. Idioms include \"kid around\" (tease) and \"you're kidding\" (joke).Etymology and OriginOrigin: Derived from Scandinavian roots (Old Norse kith), referring to a young goat.Evolution: The term was adapted in the late 16th century, likely referencing the energetic, \"bouncy\" nature of goats to describe children.First Use: Noun usage as a young goat dates back to the 12th century, while its informal usage for a human child began around the 1590s.Contextual UsageInformal Human Usage: A child, teenager, or young person.Familiar Address: Used in direct address to someone regardless of age (e.g., \"Listen, kid...\").Goat: A young goat, specifically, or leather made from its skin (kidskin).Verb (Teasing): To fool, trick, joke, or tease someone.Synonyms and Related TermsChild/Youngster: Child, young person, baby boy/girl, youngster, youth, lad, lass, tot.Teasing/Joking: Banter, fool, joke, tease, mock, spoof, rib, roast.Common Idioms and Phrases\"Just kidding\" / \"Only kidding\": Used to indicate a previous statement was a joke.\"Kid around\": To act playfully or tease.\"Kid-friendly\": Designed for children.\"Kid brother/sister\": A younger sibling.Contextual ExamplesAs a child: \"She has two kids.\"As a verb: \"You're kidding me!\"As a goat: \"The kid is only two weeks old.\"",
